  "worked_solution": "Notice that the leading coefficient is $3.$ So, we take out a factor of $3$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n3({q}^{2}-3q+5)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{q}^{2}-3q+5\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{q}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot q+5\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $5$ last:\n\n$$\n{{q}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot q+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}}_{\u23df}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+5\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(q-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+5\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(q-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{11}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3({q}^{2}-3q+5) & =3({(q-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{11}{4}) \\\\  & =3{(q-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+3\\cdot \\frac{11}{4} \\\\  & =3{(q-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{33}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
